Build the Objective-C sources with GCC and the Mac OS X 10.6 SDK.

GCC has no ARC, blocks, @autoreleasepool, object literals or nullability
qualifiers, and the 10.6 SDK declares most accessors as plain methods.

- utils_macos.m, macos_menu.m, libwolv fs_macos.m: message syntax instead of
  dot syntax, NSAutoreleasePool instead of @autoreleasepool, CFDictionaryCreate
  instead of a dictionary literal, dispatch_async_f instead of a block.
  Functions creating autoreleased objects set up their own pool, since there
  is none outside of GLFW's event processing on 10.6.
- Guard APIs newer than 10.6 by SDK version (hidden/transparent titlebars,
  full screen style mask, backing scale factor, descriptorWithBundleIdentifier:,
  renamed AppKit constants). UNUserNotificationCenter toasts are only built
  with blocks support on the 10.14+ SDK.
- Use SRefCon for the Apple Event handler refcon, it is an SInt32 on 32-bit.
- Pass menu shortcut characters by value instead of reading the first two
  bytes of an int, which are its high half on big-endian hosts.
- Link CoreText and UserNotifications only when they are separate frameworks.
- Compile libimhex's Objective-C sources as gnu11 with GCC, which defaults to
  gnu89 for Objective-C and rejects the C99 for-loop declarations in
  utils_macos.m.
- Default to native window decorations before OS X 10.10.
- Don't crash at startup when CTFontManagerCopyAvailableFontFamilyNames()
  returns NULL, as it can on 10.6; enumerate the available font collection
  instead.

Applies on top of patch-macports.diff. Behaviour with newer SDKs is unchanged.

